{"id":"8b21fc2d-50f9-42f4-a156-d1689b4f2835","title":"Architecture & Delivery Plan — SFN-21: Build Super Console as the modular self-hosted administration control plane","content":"# Architecture & Delivery Plan — <issue id="78416064-ba70-4e5c-92b0-6ec96a38c2b0" href="https://linear.app/21n/issue/SFN-21/build-super-console-as-the-modular-self-hosted-administration-control">SFN-21</issue>

[Back to SFN-21](<https://linear.app/21n/issue/SFN-21/build-super-console-as-the-modular-self-hosted-administration-control>)

## 1. Architecture objective

Implement Super Console as a self-hosted modular control plane whose UI, REST/OpenAPI, TypeScript SDK, and MCP surfaces are projections of one validated function-owned capability registry.

The architecture must make three invariants mechanically true:

1. a disabled module is not discoverable or addressable;
2. all surfaces share authorization, validation, domain execution, and audit semantics;
3. the console composes domain services but never becomes their source of business logic.

## 2. Repository layout

```text
superconsole/
  src/
    lib/
      components/            # console composites built only from UIFn
      server/                # registry, request context, policy, dispatch, audit
      modules/               # presentation adapters for registered capabilities
    routes/
      (console)/             # authenticated SvelteKit operator routes
      api/admin/v1/          # versioned REST dispatch and OpenAPI
      mcp/                   # McpFn transport/adapter entry
  admin-sdk/                 # TypeScript client package
  tests/                     # unit, contract, integration, UI
  .conduct/SFN-21/           # source-controlled architecture and delivery record
```

Function-specific administration contracts live with their owning functions where practical. Shared transport-neutral primitives live in a shared `@superfunctions/admin` package if repository inspection confirms that is the least-coupled placement. Super Console imports contracts and adapters; functions must not depend on the SvelteKit application.

## 3. Layer model

### Layer A — domain administration service

Each function owns:

* resources and stable identifiers;
* list/get/create/update/delete or domain-specific actions;
* input/output schemas;
* scope requirements;
* permission requirements;
* idempotency requirements;
* audit metadata;
* pagination/filter/sort capabilities;
* health/readiness signals;
* links to related resources;
* an adapter interface to the function's real services/storage.

No framework, Svelte, REST, or MCP types enter this layer.

### Layer B — capability manifest

A versioned manifest declares:

* module ID, version, title, maturity, required/optional classification;
* dependencies and nested ownership;
* navigation entries and resource routes;
* operations with method, path, schemas, permissions, scope, mutation risk, confirmation, idempotency, audit action, and handler binding;
* search providers, dashboard cards, health checks, and event types;
* optional UIFn presentation adapters.

Manifests are validated at process startup. Duplicate IDs/routes/tools, dependency cycles, missing required functions, unsafe mutations, and inconsistent scopes fail closed.

### Layer C — shared administration kernel

The kernel provides:

* validated deployment registry;
* request context and tenancy resolution;
* AuthFn identity/session/service credential integration;
* policy evaluation and permission enforcement;
* schema validation and normalized errors;
* cursor pagination;
* idempotency store;
* operation dispatch;
* audit event creation;
* OpenAPI projection;
* MCP tool projection through McpFn;
* global resource search projection;
* structured observability through WatchFn/LogFn.

The kernel is transport-neutral and independently tested.

### Layer D — exposure adapters

* SvelteKit UI and server actions.
* REST under `/api/admin/v1`.
* OpenAPI JSON under a stable versioned endpoint.
* TypeScript admin client.
* McpFn transport and generated tools.

Each adapter resolves the same operation by registry ID and delegates to the same kernel dispatcher.

## 4. Deployment and module registration

A typed server-only configuration explicitly registers modules and required adapters. Environment variables may point to configuration but may not silently enable domain modules.

Example conceptual form:

```ts
createSuperConsole({
  tenancy: { mode: "workspace-project-environment" },
  modules: [
    authAdmin({ service: authService }),
    watchAdmin({ service: watchService, catch: catchService, logs: logService }),
    ciAdmin({ service: ciService }),
  ],
});
```

The validated registry produces:

* enabled module set;
* route matcher;
* operation dispatch table;
* navigation tree;
* OpenAPI paths/components;
* MCP tool list;
* TypeScript client capability typing;
* dependency/health report.

A direct request for a disabled module returns a stable not-found response, not forbidden, so deployment composition is not leaked.

## 5. Tenancy and request context

Canonical hierarchy:

```text
installation
└── organization/workspace
    └── project
        └── environment
```

Request context includes actor, actor type, workspace, project, environment, role grants, authentication method, request/correlation ID, locale, and optional idempotency key.

Scopes are explicit:

* installation;
* workspace;
* project;
* environment.

A capability declares the minimum scope it consumes. Adapters validate referenced resources against the active scope. Cross-scope operations require an explicit capability and permission.

## 6. Authentication and authorization

AuthFn powers interactive sessions, service credentials, users, organizations/workspaces, membership, roles, API keys, OAuth, and MFA integration.

Authorization flow:

1. authenticate actor;
2. resolve requested scope and membership;
3. locate enabled operation;
4. evaluate declared permissions and contextual policy;
5. validate input;
6. enforce idempotency/confirmation where required;
7. execute domain service;
8. record audit result;
9. return normalized response.

Default roles may include installation admin, workspace admin, project admin, operator, developer, support, security auditor, billing admin, and read-only viewer. Custom roles are permission sets, not conditionals embedded in pages.

UI affordances are policy-aware, but hiding controls never replaces server enforcement.

## 7. Audit and observability

Every mutation emits an append-only audit envelope with actor, scope, module, operation, target references, sanitized input summary, outcome, timestamp, request ID, idempotency key, source surface, and policy decision. Sensitive values and secrets are never copied into audit payloads.

REST, SDK, console, and MCP return the same audit event ID for a successful mutation. Failed authorization and high-risk validation failures are also observable according to policy.

WatchFn is the console observability backbone:

* LogFn supplies structured logs;
* CatchFn contributes feedback/bug inbox;
* metrics, traces, errors, replays, monitors, alerts, and incidents surface inside WatchFn;
* generic console health signals link to their owning module.

## 8. REST and OpenAPI

REST uses resource-oriented paths under `/api/admin/v1`, plus explicit action endpoints when the domain operation is not CRUD.

Contract requirements:

* JSON request/response;
* stable error envelope;
* cursor pagination;
* typed filter/sort fields;
* request/correlation ID;
* idempotency header on replayable mutations;
* optimistic concurrency token where needed;
* explicit scope headers or canonical route parameters;
* API version and capability version metadata;
* structured deprecation metadata.

OpenAPI is generated from enabled manifests and includes security schemes, schemas, errors, pagination, idempotency, permissions, operation IDs, and module tags. Disabled paths are absent.

## 9. TypeScript admin SDK

The SDK wraps REST and provides:

* typed client construction and authentication;
* explicit workspace/project/environment context;
* per-module clients;
* pagination iterators;
* normalized errors;
* idempotency support;
* abort/timeout handling;
* raw-response escape hatch;
* capability discovery.

The SDK must not import SvelteKit or server domain implementations. Contract tests compare its operation IDs and schemas with generated OpenAPI.

## 10. MCP through McpFn

Each eligible operation projects to a namespaced McpFn tool with:

* deterministic name and description;
* JSON schema derived from the capability;
* active scope fields or session-bound context;
* required permissions;
* read/mutation classification;
* confirmation and idempotency metadata;
* sanitized output and audit ID.

Tool discovery includes enabled modules only. MCP invokes the shared dispatcher, so it cannot bypass policy, validation, tenancy, or auditing. Secrets and irreversible mutations require narrow schemas and explicit confirmation semantics.

## 11. SvelteKit/UIFn interface

Super Console uses UIFn for tokens, typography, layout primitives, navigation, forms, tables, tabs, badges, dialogs, drawers, command palette, notifications, charts, code/log viewers, empty/loading/error states, and responsive behavior.

Rules enforced by code review/lint/tests:

* no parallel component toolkit;
* no locally recreated UIFn primitive;
* no hard-coded design tokens where a UIFn token exists;
* no customer portal component reused as operator administration without an operator-specific UIFn contract;
* any missing or broken primitive is fixed in `uifn/` and tested there.

SvelteKit owns routing and server boundaries. Tailwind is configured through UIFn tokens/preset. Module pages consume registry-backed view models rather than importing arbitrary domain storage.

## 12. Module map

### Required standalone modules

* AuthFn
* BillFn
* BrowseFn
* CiFn
* CmsFn
* CompFn
* DataFn
* FileFn
* FlowFn
* HostFn
* MailFn
* MemoryFn
* PlugFn
* RecFn
* SearchFn
* SecFn
* SendFn
* TestFn
* UserFn
* WatchFn

### Nested modules

* WatchFn owns CatchFn bug inbox and LogFn log analysis.
* CmsFn owns ContentFn datasets, documents, drafts, revisions, assets, and publishing.
* CiFn dashboard behavior is migrated into the Super Console module and its React shell is no longer the canonical operator experience.

### Optional modules

ApiFn, BotFn, CacheFn, DocsFn, LangFn, ProbeFn.

## 13. Delivery tracks

### Track 1 — foundation

* Isolated exact-head worktree and `superconsole/.conduct/SFN-21/` bundle.
* Shared admin contract types and manifest validator.
* Registry, dependency resolution, config, request context, errors, pagination, idempotency, policy, audit.
* REST/OpenAPI, SDK, and McpFn projections.
* Test harness and representative in-memory adapters.

### Track 2 — shell and visual system

* SvelteKit/Tailwind shell.
* UIFn audit, fixes, Svelte primitives, tokens, and enforcement.
* Authentication boundary, selectors, navigation, search, notifications, resource patterns, action confirmation, audit receipt.
* Responsive and accessibility checks.

### Track 3 — backbone modules

* AuthFn and audit.
* WatchFn with CatchFn and LogFn.
* CiFn dashboard migration.
* CmsFn with ContentFn.

### Track 4 — platform modules

* PlugFn, SendFn, MailFn, BillFn, FileFn.
* DataFn, FlowFn, HostFn, SecFn, SearchFn.

### Track 5 — remaining verticals

* BrowseFn, CompFn, MemoryFn, RecFn, TestFn, UserFn.
* ApiFn, BotFn, CacheFn, DocsFn, LangFn, ProbeFn.

### Track 6 — hardening

* Cross-surface contract tests.
* Tenant isolation and permission matrix.
* Disabled-module exposure tests.
* Build/typecheck/lint/unit/integration/UI verification.
* Self-hosted deployment, backup, upgrade, and operations documentation.
* Coherent local commits and final source/Linear readback.

Parallel work must use non-overlapping file ownership and converge on the shared contracts before integration.

## 14. Testing strategy

### Unit

* manifest validation and dependency cycles;
* registry filtering;
* route/tool collision detection;
* permission and scope decisions;
* pagination, errors, idempotency, audit redaction;
* OpenAPI and MCP projection.

### Contract

* function adapter conforms to declared operations;
* REST/OpenAPI/SDK/MCP operation parity;
* schemas accept/reject identical payloads;
* audit IDs and error codes are preserved across surfaces.

### Integration

* authenticated UI and API flows;
* workspace/project/environment isolation;
* disabled module returns not found on all surfaces;
* safe and destructive mutations;
* representative operations for every module;
* CiFn runs, job detail, log streaming/display, artifact access;
* WatchFn error/log/feedback correlation;
* CmsFn draft/publish and ContentFn resource flows.

### UI and accessibility

* keyboard navigation, focus order, labels, contrast, reduced motion;
* loading, empty, partial, error, unauthorized, forbidden, and not-found states;
* narrow and wide viewports;
* UIFn-only dependency enforcement.

### Security

* tenant escape attempts;
* confused-deputy/service credential cases;
* object-level authorization;
* secret redaction;
* replay/idempotency;
* MCP mutation confirmation;
* audit tamper resistance assumptions.

## 15. Documentation bundle

Create under `superconsole/.conduct/SFN-21/`:

* `README.md` — index and decision summary;
* `product-rationale.md`;
* `architecture.md`;
* `capability-inventory.md`;
* `module-contract.md`;
* `rest-openapi.md`;
* `typescript-sdk.md`;
* `mcp.md`;
* `security-tenancy-rbac.md`;
* `uifn-rules.md`;
* `cifn-migration.md`;
* `testing.md`;
* `self-hosting-operations.md`;
* `delivery-plan.md`;
* `decisions.md`;
* `verification.md`.

Docs must describe implemented behavior, not aspirational placeholders, and must be updated when verification changes the design.

## 16. Commit plan

Keep all work local with coherent commits, for example:

1. architecture and administration contracts;
2. registry, policy, REST/OpenAPI, SDK, and MCP;
3. UIFn primitives and Super Console shell;
4. backbone modules and CiFn migration;
5. platform modules;
6. remaining required and optional modules;
7. tests, self-hosting, and final documentation.

Exact boundaries may change to preserve buildable commits, but generated artifacts and their sources belong together. No push or pull request.

## 17. Completion gate

Delivery is complete only when:

* all enabled surfaces are derived from one registry;
* the full required and optional module set is implemented;
* representative real operations exist for each module;
* UIFn is the exclusive visual system;
* disabled modules are absent everywhere;
* policy, tenancy, audit, REST, SDK, and MCP parity tests pass;
* CiFn workflows operate in the SvelteKit shell;
* self-hosted operation is documented;
* local verification evidence is recorded;
* commits are coherent and remain local.","icon":null,"color":null,"url":"https://linear.app/21n/document/architecture-and-delivery-plan-sfn-21-build-super-console-as-the-cdda3a6882be","slugId":"cdda3a6882be","createdAt":"2026-08-13T01:42:20.093Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-13T01:42:21.069Z","archivedAt":null,"creator":{"id":"3a6ed003-6380-47ef-9e7b-b2b01ebddc6a","name":"Aravind"},"updatedBy":{"id":"3a6ed003-6380-47ef-9e7b-b2b01ebddc6a","name":"Aravind"},"project":null,"initiative":null,"team":null,"issue":{"id":"SFN-21","title":"Build Super Console as the modular self-hosted administration control plane"}}

